title: Rollback
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: overview
aliases: rollback
summary: A rollback moves HEAD to any point of the history tree — back, forward, onto another branch — undoing and redoing on the way, files included.

A ''rollback'' ''navigates'' the history tree: it moves HEAD to a target operation — an
ancestor (back in time), a descendant (a redo), or a node on another branch — undoing
the operations between here and there and re-applying those between there and the
target. Nothing is deleted: the branch left behind stays reachable, and a write after a
rollback starts a new branch ([[History tree and HEAD]]).

It is "take me back to how things were at lunchtime" — everything in between is undone
because it is in the way. To undo one change and keep the rest, revert it
([[Revert]]); to take back your last change whatever the watcher did since, undo
([[Undo and redo]]).

Files come along: moves made through metafolder are moved back, content that went
through the trash comes back from it, and a redone trashing sends it there again
([[Filesystem coordination]]). What cannot be undone on disk is left as it is, and the
metadata is corrected to match it.

